@page "/agent-mode"

<PageTitle>Agent mode - Linq2GraphQL</PageTitle>

<h1 class="page-heading">Agent mode <span class="badge bg-orange-lt align-middle">Preview</span></h1>
<p class="lead text-secondary">
    Agent mode lets an AI agent query your API by writing GraphQL, limited to the operations you allow. You
    decide in typed C# what the agent may use and what it is told; every query it writes is checked against that
    scope before it is sent.
</p>

<section class="doc-section">
    <h2 id="why">Why</h2>
    <p>
        There are two common ways to give an agent access to an API, and both have problems. Handing it the whole
        schema spends tokens on everything, exposes operations nobody chose, and leaves the agent with descriptions
        written for developers. Writing one tool per question is precise, but slow to build and rigid.
    </p>
    <p>
        Agent mode sits in between. The agent writes its own queries, so it can answer questions you didn't
        anticipate, but it only sees a curated part of the schema, with your descriptions and examples, and it can't
        step outside it. When a question turns out to be frequent or sensitive, you can still give it a dedicated tool.
    </p>
</section>

<section class="doc-section">
    <h2 id="setup">Setup</h2>
    <p>
        Generate the client with <code>--agent</code>. The client then carries the schema it was generated from, so
        the agent tools need no introspection at runtime:
    </p>
    <CodeBlock Language="shell" Code="@Setup"/>
    <p>
        The tools are <code>AIFunction</code>s from
        <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/dotnet/ai/microsoft-extensions-ai" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Microsoft.Extensions.AI</a>,
        so they work with any chat client built on it, such as Azure OpenAI, OpenAI or Ollama:
    </p>
    <CodeBlock Code="@Basic"/>
    <p>
        The chat client needs function invocation turned on (<code>.AsBuilder().UseFunctionInvocation().Build()</code>)
        so the model's tool calls are actually run.
    </p>
    <p>
        Calling <code>AsAgentTools</code> on a client generated without <code>--agent</code> doesn't compile, because
        the client doesn't carry its schema. Either regenerate with <code>--agent</code>, or pass the schema as SDL,
        for example a file exported from the server: <code>client.AsAgentTools(sdl, o =&gt; ...)</code>.
    </p>
</section>

<section class="doc-section">
    <h2 id="tools">The tools</h2>
    <div class="table-responsive">
        <table class="table table-vcenter card-table border">
            <thead>
            <tr><th>Tool</th><th>What the agent gets</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
            <tr><td><code>graphql_query</code></td><td>Runs a query and returns the data, or the errors in a form the agent can act on. Its description lists the allowed operations, types and examples.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>describe_operation</code></td><td>Everything needed to call an operation: arguments, the return type, a ready-made starter query and the examples that use it.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>describe_type</code></td><td>Full detail for a type, plus a summary of the types its fields return.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>search_schema</code></td><td>Matching operations, types, fields and examples, for when the agent doesn't know a name.</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </div>
    <p>
        The three schema tools answer locally, without calling the server. The agent sees a simplified schema:
        connections are collapsed to a paged list with the paging pattern spelled out, and HotChocolate filter
        inputs are summarised, which on large schemas is most of the size.
    </p>
</section>

<section class="doc-section">
    <h2 id="scope">Choosing what the agent may use</h2>
    <p>Everything is referenced through the generated client, so a schema rename breaks the build instead of the agent:</p>
    <CodeBlock Code="@Scope"/>
    <div class="table-responsive">
        <table class="table table-vcenter card-table border">
            <thead>
            <tr><th>Option</th><th>What it does</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
            <tr><td><code>AllowQuery</code></td><td>Allows one query. <code>.Describe</code> and <code>.Argument</code> tell the agent what it is for and how to use its arguments.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>AllowAllQueries().Except(...)</code></td><td>Allows every query except the ones listed, for the fastest start. Never the default; everything below still applies.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>Omit</code></td><td>Removes a field from the scope. It isn't described, can't be queried, and can't be used in filters or sorting either, so its values can't be inferred.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>Describe</code></td><td>Replaces the schema's description of a type, field or enum value with one written for the agent. Pass <code>append: true</code> to add to it instead.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>Instructions</code></td><td>General context, such as time zones or units.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>Example</code></td><td>A common task written as a typed query. It is checked by the compiler and against the scope, and shown to the agent as GraphQL. Examples are the strongest way to improve the agent's queries.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>AccessToken</code></td><td>The token for the user the agent acts for, asked for on every request.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>OnQuery</code></td><td>Called for every query the agent runs or tries to run: the GraphQL, variables, validation errors, server errors and duration.</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </div>
    <p>
        A configuration that doesn't match the schema, such as an unknown name or a description of an omitted
        field, throws <code>AgentConfigurationException</code> when <code>AsAgentTools</code> runs, not halfway through
        a conversation.
    </p>
</section>

<section class="doc-section">
    <h2 id="validation">What is checked before a query is sent</h2>
    <ul>
        <li>Only allowed operations, and only queries: <code>graphql_query</code> never sends a mutation or subscription.</li>
        <li>Every selected field must exist and not be omitted, including inside fragments.</li>
        <li>Arguments, input objects, filters, enum values and variable values are validated too, so an omitted field can't be reached through a variable.</li>
        <li>Introspection (<code>__schema</code>, <code>__type</code>) is blocked.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
        A rejected query gets every error at once, each saying what is wrong and what is available instead, with
        "did you mean" suggestions for near-miss names. The agent can usually fix the query on the next try without
        a round trip to the server.
    </p>
</section>

<section class="doc-section">
    <h2 id="security">Security</h2>
    <p>
        The client keeps the agent inside the scope you configured, whatever it is prompted to do: a disallowed
        operation or an omitted field never leaves the process. It doesn't replace authorization on the server.
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li>
            <strong>The server is the boundary.</strong> Protect sensitive fields with authorization on the server, so
            they stay protected for every client, not just this one.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Give the agent a narrow token.</strong> Set <code>AccessToken</code> to an on-behalf-of token for the
            current user, ideally with a scope meant for agents, and create the tools per user or conversation. The
            agent then never sees more than both the user and the scope allow.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Prompt injection can still read what the scope allows.</strong> Text the agent reads can make it
            query and repeat anything within its scope. The narrow token and the allow-list limit what that can be.
        </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
        The first version is query-only. Mutations are planned, with approval and their own safety design.
    </p>
</section>

<section class="doc-section">
    <h2 id="sample">Try it</h2>
    <p>
        <a href="https://github.com/Linq2GraphQL/Linq2GraphQL.Client/tree/main/test/Linq2GraphQL.Agent.Sample" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><code>test/Linq2GraphQL.Agent.Sample</code></a>
        in the repository runs an agent against the test server, as a chat or as an evaluation over a set of
        questions. It works with Azure AI Foundry through your Azure sign-in, or any OpenAI-compatible endpoint such
        as Ollama. The <a href="https://github.com/Linq2GraphQL/Linq2GraphQL.Client/blob/main/docs/design/agent-mode.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">design document</a>
        explains the reasoning behind agent mode and what is planned next.
    </p>
</section>

@code {
    private const string Setup = """
        Linq2GraphQL https://example.com/graphql --agent -c=SampleClient -n=Sample -o=Generated
        dotnet add package Linq2GraphQL.Client.AI --prerelease
        """;

    private const string Basic = """
        var tools = sampleClient.AsAgentTools(o =>
        {
            o.AllowQuery(c => c.Query.Customers).Describe("All customers with their orders.");
            o.AllowQuery(c => c.Query.Orders).Describe("Orders, paged and filterable.");
        });

        var options = new ChatOptions { Tools = [..tools] };
        var response = await chatClient.GetResponseAsync("Which customers have no orders?", options);
        """;

    private const string Scope = """
        var tools = sampleClient.AsAgentTools(o =>
        {
            o.AllowQuery(c => c.Query.Customers).Describe("All customers with their orders.");
            o.AllowQuery(c => c.Query.Orders)
                .Describe("Orders, paged and filterable.")
                .Argument("first", "At most 50.");

            o.Describe<Customer>("A customer account.");
            o.Describe<Customer>(c => c.Status, "ACTIVE customers are currently invoiced.");
            o.Omit<Customer>(c => c.Address);
            o.Instructions("All dates are UTC.");

            o.Example("Customer statuses", "Every customer's name and status",
                c => c.Query.Customers().Select(e => e.Select(x => new { x.CustomerName, x.Status })));

            o.AccessToken = ct => tokens.GetAgentTokenForUserAsync(user, ct);
            o.OnQuery(log => logger.LogInformation("{Query} sent: {Sent} {Errors}",
                log.Query, log.WasSent, log.ValidationErrors));
        });
        """;
}
